Scripted input and a controllable clock: the event recorder's transport, without its tape.

WHY. Every interaction this harness can drive today goes through XTEST keystrokes into the stock
binary's graphical console. It works, and it is the only path that completes a guard row, but it
is flaky over a long run and its timing is wall-clock. The text-console build (patch 0001) fixed
the typing and gave a deterministic instruction countdown, and then could not finish a single
interaction: it parks forever at a modal print, in EVERY guard mode, because the game clock does
not advance while the debugger holds stdin. An A/B against the stock binary settled that the
build, not the game and not the guards, was at fault.

ScummVM already contains the two mechanisms that fix this, welded to an oracle we cannot use:

  * EventRecorder IS a Common::EventSource. Its pollEvent() supplies events to the engine during
    playback and warps the cursor to match -- deterministic input through the engine's own
    pipeline. No X server, no window focus, no dropped keystroke.
  * processMillis() intercepts getMillis(), returns _fakeTimer, and drives the timer manager
    itself. The engine's clock becomes ours.

Its oracle is a screenshot MD5 compared against a recorded tape. That answers "is this the same
as last time" -- a REGRESSION question. Record a session in which a guard is broken and the
replay passes forever, because the tape IS the spec. This project needs the CONFORMANCE question:
does the game behave the way the spec derived from the emitted patch source says it must. And the
KQ5 lite estate is not 25 tapes; it is 25 PARAMETERISATIONS of one shape (item, mode, warn word,
room), authored from a site table.

So: keep the transport, drop the tape. Three of the four pieces already exist -- expectations
(tools/probes/_sites.py), state setup (the SCI console), verdicts (`has:`, the warn bit,
`said()`). This adds the fourth.

WHAT IT ADDS.

    --script-input=FILE        drive the game from a text input script
    --script-clock-step=NUM    virtual ms per getMillis() query (default 10)
    --script-seed=NUM          fixed RNG seed, so a run is reproducible
    --script-trace=NUM         print the virtual clock every NUM ms
    --script-shared            let real mouse/keyboard through alongside the script
    --script-realtime          keep delayMillis, so a run plays at watchable speed

and a `script` command in the debugger, which is where the interesting steps are actually
authored -- WHERE to click is an nsRect read out of the live VM, and half these targets are
Actors, which walk, so no file written before boot can know it:

    script                     the clock, the step rate, how much is pending
    script clock <ms>          change the clock rate
    script trace <ms>          print the clock every <ms> of virtual time (0 = off)
    script clear               forget everything not yet fired
    script load <file>         load a file, rebased onto NOW
    script add t=+500 click 141 124

A script is one step per line; `#` comments; `t=` absolute or `t=+` relative virtual ms:

    clock 10
    t=1200 click 141 124          move, press, release 100ms later
    t=+400 rclick 141 124
    t=+250 key escape             a name from the table, or a single character
    t=2500 mark offered           prints "[script] t=2500 mark offered" on stdout
    t=3000 break                  hands control to the SCI console at a known virtual time
    t=9000 quit                   ends the run -- a script that never quits never terminates

THE CLOCK IS THE POINT. _fakeTimer advances by _clockStep for every getMillis() the engine
performs, and by nothing else. It is therefore deterministic -- the same script over the same
game gives the same virtual times whatever the machine is doing -- and it cannot be starved.
Every game-side wait is a loop that queries the time, so every such loop terminates in a bounded
number of queries. That is exactly the failure that stopped the text-console build. Audio rides
along: the recorder's NullMixerManager is pumped from the same place, so a wait-for-speech
advances with no audio device.

`mark` and `break` are the answer to the other open thread. On the keystroke path the guard
fires but the warn bit does not read as set, and the refusal text turns up in the NEXT sample's
window -- the arm completes asynchronously with respect to sampling. A probe now puts a mark a
known number of VIRTUAL milliseconds after the click and is handed the console back at the very
next step, so the sample is taken at a stated point in game time rather than after a wall-clock
sleep.

`--script-trace` exists because two failures look identical from outside and mean opposite
things: a frozen clock, and a clock that runs while the engine has stopped polling events. Marks
alone cannot tell them apart -- they print from the event poll -- so the trace prints the clock
from processMillis instead. It earned its place immediately: it showed the clock stopped dead
during a hang that had been attributed to time starvation, which is the reverse of the story.

⭐ WHAT THIS FOUND, once it worked. A backtrace taken from INSIDE a guard's message box -- which
only a `break` step makes possible -- says the box is `Dialog::doit` polling kGetEvent. It waits
for INPUT, not for time. So the previous session's diagnosis, that the game clock does not
advance while the debugger holds stdin, was the wrong cause: no clock control ends that box, and
the arm's remaining statements stay unreached until something dismisses it. Return or a click
does; space does not. With that, the first guard row ever completed without a person at the
keyboard: KQ5 LA6, both attempts, state and dialogue.

WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO. No recording file, no screenshot oracle, no game-hash gate (a
script is written against the emitted source, and every patch build rewrites the game's scripts).
Script mode also keeps the REAL save manager, where the recorder swaps in a fake one, because
whether mode survives save/restore is a question a run has to be able to ask.

FOR LOCAL DEBUGGING ONLY. Apply on top of 0001-0003.
APPLY WITH: (cd tools/scummvm-ref && git apply ../scummvm-patches/0004-*.patch)

